First, the disclaimer:  in principle, I agree with virtually 100% of what
you said in your message.  However, I think that certain things in nethack
could be toned down a bit.  Take, for instance, the example of how I used
the black pudding trick...

For starters, I created a fairly simple unix script.  What this script did
was to start up a nethack game, redirecting the output to a file.  It would
then immediately tell the game to do an inventory, and then save out.  
Next, it ran a quick scan on the resulting output file to see if the 
character produced (I used wizards for this - it doesn't really work with
anybody else) had a scroll of scare monster.  If the character didn't, it
would delete the save file.  Otherwise, it renamed the save file to a
numbered file and went on to find another one.  After a few hours, I would
have several files, character.1, character.2, etc., all of which had
scrolls of scare monster.

I would then quickly go through these save files (renaming them back to
the character save file - 3139Endiel.Z), and explore about the first four
levels on each, until I found a game that had a sink and an altar on the
same level.

Next, the Elbereths got engraved at strategic places around the level, and
the sink got kicked until it produced one (1) black pudding.  Then, via
judicious use of Elbereth, as well as strategic maneuvering, the black
pudding would be maneuvered into the room with the altar.  The scroll of
scare monster then either got dropped on or beside the altar, and I stood
on the scroll killing black puddings to my heart's content, occasionally
sacrificing or eating corpses and in general really pleasing my deity.

Remember:  By using a scroll of scare monster, I could guarantee that the
black puddings could NEVER hit me while I was standing on it.  Venturing
off of it was extremely dangerous indeed, but as long as I stayed on it,
I was fine.  Also, as long as I didn't a) save and restore the game, or 
b) exit the level and come back, the black puddings would keep splitting -
even if I had already tripped the genocide flag by killing more than 100
of them.

Next, after killing a couple hundred of the black puddings, I took
a wand of polymorph that I had picked up along the way (probably from
some of the stuff that had been produced when the black puddings got 
killed).  I then ventured off of my scroll onto a spot with about 90 
black pudding corpses, and immediately engraved Elbereth.  I then 
wandered back, and zapped the wand of polymorph until I had 90 food
rations.  The food rations then got moved onto the scroll of scare 
monster with me, so that I wouldn't even have to leave it for food.

Now, I was ready to kick in the final phase of my process.  You see, 
I was doing all of this via modem, from my PC (and it was within the
area in which any single call is a flat rate, regardless of how long
I was on the line - I'm sure Illinois Bell hated what I did next).  
My terminal program has a very powerful script language - so I wrote 
myself a script.  What this script did was simple.  It checked to see
if there was a black pudding next to me, and if there was, it attacked
it.  If there was not (which was basically never by this point), it
simply waited a turn and tried again.  If it ever detected that I was
becoming hungry, it would make me eat.  If it ever detected that I
had, for some reason, become weak, or if it detected that I had been
hit by another creature (perhaps a humanoid able to ignore the scroll),
it would immediately stop and ring an alarm for five seconds.

I then simply let the script run.  Using this method, I was able to 
rack up over two million points in less than a twenty four hour period -
without playing!  I let the computer do it for me, and only had to
help out every once in a while when the computer got confused.  The
only reason I stopped then was because a) I got bored, and b) I was
afraid my wife-to-be would get pissed at me for tying up the phone.

Needless to say, the fact that this can occur is a problem with nethack.  
My personal suggestion is a very simple one.  This type of abuse can be
stopped quite easily - no, we don't have to take out black puddings or
other reproducing monsters (the same trick can be accomplished with 
brown puddings, incubi/succubi, and gremlins, albeit with modifications).  
No, we don't have to take out the ability for a sink to produce black
puddings.  Just make it impossible for a black pudding to split after
the genocide bit has been flipped.  If, when a split were to occur, the
game were to check the genocide bit, and then not allow the split because
of that, this type of scenario would be impossible.

Anyways, I've probably rambled on way too long.  Comments?  Suggestions?
(No, I'm not endorsing doing what I did - it was mainly an exercise to
see if it were possible, and in programming in the script language.)

